    {
      "short": "Plaintext",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "title": "Passwords in a column",
      "body": [
        "<span class=\"term\">Authentication</span> answers one question: <i>are you really who you claim to be?</i> <b>Nestegg</b>, a savings app, started the simple way — a users table with the password stored as typed, compared with <code>==</code> on login.",
        "It works. But anyone who ever reads that table — a backup on a lost laptop, a SQL injection, a curious insider — now has every customer's password. And since people reuse passwords, also their email and their bank."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warn",
        "text": "Never store a password you can read back. If you can recover it, so can whoever steals your database."
      },
      "metrics": {
        "leak": "all passwords",
        "stuffing": "—",
        "mfa": "no"
      },
