    {
      "short": "Fast hash",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "title": "Hashed — but too quickly",
      "body": [
        "A <span class=\"term\">hash</span> is a one-way function: easy to compute, impossible to reverse. Nestegg now stores <code>sha256(password)</code> and compares hashes. Better — until the table leaks.",
        "General-purpose hashes are built to be <b>fast</b>, so an attacker can try billions of guesses per second. And with no salt, everyone with the password <code>Summer2019!</code> has the same hash: crack it once, and every matching row falls at the same time."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "~ Passwords stored as unsalted SHA-256",
        "+ Database dump leaked"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "leak": "most passwords",
        "stuffing": "—",
        "mfa": "no"
      },
