    {
      "short": "Slow + salt",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "title": "Slow, salted hashing",
      "body": [
        "Use a <span class=\"term\">password hashing function</span> — <b>argon2id</b> or <b>bcrypt</b> — that is deliberately slow and memory-hungry, with a tunable cost. 150 ms is nothing for one login but cripples an attacker trying billions.",
        "Each user also gets a random <span class=\"term\">salt</span> stored next to the hash. Identical passwords now produce different hashes, so precomputed tables are useless and each account must be attacked separately. Only weak passwords remain crackable, slowly."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "~ Hash with argon2id, tuned to ~150 ms",
        "+ Random salt per user",
        "~ Rehash old SHA-256 rows at next login"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "leak": "weak ones, slowly",
        "stuffing": "—",
        "mfa": "no"
      },
