      "short": "Sessions",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Remembering who logged in",
      "body": [
        "Nobody wants to type a password on every click. After a successful login, the server needs to remember the result. The classic way is a <b>session</b>.",
        "Auth creates a random <span class=\"term\">session ID</span>, stores <code>sid → user, expiry</code> in a fast store and sends the sid in a <code>HttpOnly; Secure</code> cookie. Every request, the API looks the sid up. Logging out — or kicking out a stolen session — is one delete."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "+ Session store",
        "+ Session cookie on every request",
        "~ API checks each sid"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "leak": "weak ones, slowly",
        "stuffing": "—",
