    {
      "short": "Tokens",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "title": "Signed tokens instead",
      "body": [
        "A <span class=\"term\">JWT</span> flips this around: Auth signs a small token saying <i>user 81, expires 10:15</i>. Any service holding the public key can verify it without calling anyone, which suits many services and mobile apps.",
        "The catch is revocation: a valid token works until it expires, even after logout or theft. So keep access tokens short-lived (minutes) and pair them with a longer <b>refresh token</b> that Auth can check — and revoke — when issuing new ones."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "- Session lookups from the API",
        "+ Short-lived signed access tokens",
        "+ Revocable refresh tokens"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "leak": "weak ones, slowly",
        "stuffing": "—",
        "mfa": "no"
      },
